<description>
**Problem:** Benchmarks embed [block 413567](https://mempool.space/block/413567), a nearly 1 MB binary fixture mined in 2016 before SegWit v0 and Taproot. Its transaction and script mix is fixed. Replacing it with a newer block, as in #32457, would retain a binary fixture and a single fixed workload.

**Fix:** Replace the fixture with `bench/block_generator.{h,cpp}`, which generates deterministic regtest benchmark blocks from configurable recipes and returns a block or serialized bytes. Generated undo data lets the JSON benchmarks exercise fee and prevout conversion. The inputs use synthetic outpoints, so these blocks are not intended to connect to a chain.

**Reproducer:** Run the generator suite and sanity-check the benchmarks:

```bash
rm -rf build && \
cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_BENCH=ON && \
cmake --build build -j$(nproc) && \
build/bin/test_bitcoin -t bench_block_generator_tests && \
build/bin/bench_bitcoin -sanity-check -filter='DeserializeBlockTest|CheckBlockTest|LoadExternalBlockFile|WriteBlockBench|ReadBlockBench|ReadRawBlockBench|BlockToJsonVerbosity1|BlockToJsonVerbosity2|BlockToJsonVerbosity3|BlockToJsonVerboseWrite|HexStrBench'
```
</description>

<commits>
c1874d1cd6 bench: route legacy fixture through helper APIs

Bench files loaded `block413567.raw` directly, so moving off the fixture required touching each benchmark's serialization setup.
Add shared helper entry points backed by the current fixture and switch benchmark callsites to those helpers.
This keeps behavior stable while moving setup to `regtest`, where the follow-up generated-block path can find a valid nonce quickly.

Co-authored-by: hodlinator <hodlinator@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-authored-by: MarcoFalke <*~=`'#}+{/-|&$^_@721217.xyz>

43eb9a72a8 bench: switch helper to generated blocks and recipes

The helper migration still returned one fixed historical fixture block, which kept benchmark coverage narrow and blocked script-mix experiments.
Replace the fixture-backed helper with runtime block generation and `ScriptRecipe` presets so callsites can tune output mixes.
Generation uses regtest parameters, keeps blocks under the weight limit, and leaves returned validation caches clear.
The legacy preset omits witness data, and generated inputs use random outpoints rather than a connectable chain.

Add unit tests that lock in deterministic seed behavior, helper round-trip validity, and the default benchmark block hash.
Link `bench_bitcoin` with `secp256k1` so benchmark code can include `<secp256k1.h>` and use canonical pubkey tag constants.

Co-authored-by: hodlinator <hodlinator@users.noreply.github.com>

9900578e56 bench: remove embedded `block413567.raw` fixture

After benchmarks switched to helper-generated data, the embedded `block413567.raw` fixture and its cmake raw-data wiring were no longer needed.

Co-authored-by: hodlinator <hodlinator@users.noreply.github.com>

01f023b61d bench: generate undo for JSON benchmarks

Without undo data, the block-to-JSON benchmarks skip fee and prevout work at detail verbosity.
Generate deterministic synthetic undo coins aligned with each non-coinbase input and write them so the JSON benchmarks exercise those paths.
The generated outpoints have no chain-backed UTXOs, so the undo data supports benchmark conversion rather than chain validation.
The block manager owns the generated block's index, so its JSON includes the genesis parent and chainwork.
The existing `BlockToJsonVerboseWrite` setup checks the fee and prevout fields.
This avoids another embedded binary fixture.
</commits>

